    "evidence_text": "Our model is also much more compact relative to previous approaches.\n\nOther low resource g2p systems have used a strategy of combining multiple models.\n\nwhich the various languages have similar but different and possibly contradictory spelling rules, can be seen as an extreme case of that.\n\nTherefore, a data-driven sequence-to-sequence model is a natural choice.\n\nThe model consists of two main parts: the encoder compresses each source grapheme sequence INLINEFORM0 into a fixed-length vector.\n\nThe encoder and decoder are both implemented as recurrent neural networks, which have the advantage of being able to process sequences of arbitrary length and use long histories efficiently.\n\nWe had our best results when using a bidirectional encoder, which consists of two separate encoders which process the input in forward and reverse directions.\n\nFor the attention mechanism, we used the general global attention architecture described by luong2015effective.\n\nWe implemented all models with OpenNMT  .\n\nOur hyperparameters, which we determined by experimentation, are listed in Table TABREF8 .\n\nthey built adapted models.\n\nAs is noted above, the Adapted set omits 23 languages which are in the High test set.\n\nare presented in Table TABREF24 .",
